    "filename": "QPM-003 Risk Management Plan Procedure.md",
    "text": "# QPM-003 — Risk Management Plan Procedure (ENERCON), Rev. 2 — Operating Digest\n\nThis app only produces the **Qualitative** RMP (QL-RMP, form QF-034). It never produces\nthe Quantitative RMP (QN-RMP, form QF-035) — do not use numeric 1–5 scoring or the\nquantitative formula (Risk Assessment Score = likelihood + 2×impact). If a user's\nrequest sounds like it wants quantitative/numeric scoring, say plainly that this tool\nonly does the qualitative form and proceed qualitatively.\n\n## Purpose\n\nA Risk Management Plan identifies predictable risk exposures to a project, assesses\ntheir likelihood and impact, determines compensating actions and mitigation plans, and\ncommunicates risk information to the project team, ENERCON management, the client\nteam, and client management.\n\n## What counts as a risk exposure\n\n- One specific risk per row/entry — not a vague category or a bundle of several\n  unrelated concerns.\n- 1–2 sentences or bullet points: enough detail that someone unfamiliar with the\n  project understands the risk.\n\n## Judgment calls the procedure deliberately leaves to you\n\nThe procedure defines Low/Medium/High only in general qualitative terms (below) — it\ngives no numeric thresholds, word counts, or hard rules for exactly where \"possible\"\nends and \"likely\" begins. This is intentional: qualitative risk management is\njudgment-driven, not formulaic. Calibrate to the specific project/system being\nassessed, using your own reasoning about what's realistic — don't force false\nprecision the source material doesn't support, and don't default every risk to\nMedium just because it's the \"safe middle\" choice.\n\n- **Likelihood/Probability** — Low: unlikely. Medium: possible. High: likely or very\n  likely.\n- **Impact/Consequence** — Low: low or minor impact. Medium: moderate impact. High:\n  high or catastrophic impact to the project.\n\n## Risk tiers (from the 3×3 Risk Analysis Matrix — exact grid in the QF-034 reference)\n\n- **Green (Low Risk)** — low impact; minimal oversight required; status is monitored\n  throughout the project until closed.\n- **Yellow (Medium Risk)** — some project disruption is possible; a different design\n  approach may be required; additional management attention may be needed;\n  scheduling and project funding may be impacted; may require a contingency plan\n  should the exposure become an occurrence.\n- **Red (High Risk)** — unacceptable major project disruption is likely; a different\n  design approach may be warranted; senior management attention and monitoring is\n  required; scheduling and project funding will be impacted; compensating actions and\n  contingency plans should be developed and discussed. **Any Red risk exposure must be\n  flagged explicitly** — the procedure requires the RMP to be reviewed with ENERCON\n  management and the client's management team (and communicated to subcontractor\n  management if applicable) whenever Red exposures are present.\n\n## Ownership\n\nEach risk exposure is owned by the party or parties most capable of successfully\nmanaging it — this could be ENERCON, the client, a subcontractor, or a vendor. List\nmore than one organization when genuinely shared.\n\n## Risk Impact detail requirement\n\nGreen risk exposures only need the affected areas marked (Quality / Scope / Cost /\nSchedule) — a detailed narrative is optional. Yellow and Red risk exposures REQUIRE a\ndetailed narrative of the impact in addition to marking the areas, quantified in\ndollars, man-hours, or days where that's realistically knowable from what the user has\ngiven you. Don't fabricate a dollar figure or a day count that isn't grounded in\nsomething the user told you — state the impact qualitatively instead if no\nquantification is supportable.\n\n## Compensating actions — pick one or more per risk exposure\n\n- **Avoidance [AV]** — change the plan, clarify requirements, obtain missing\n  information, add resources or schedule time, use a proven/common approach instead of\n  an unfamiliar one, avoid an unfamiliar subcontractor or vendor.\n- **Transference [T]** — shift the consequence (and ideally ownership) of the risk to\n  another party — e.g. liquidated damages, a vendor managed directly by the client.\n  Doesn't always eliminate the exposure, but is useful for financial risks.\n- **Mitigation [M]** — reduce the likelihood and/or impact toward an acceptable\n  threshold; may include a contingency plan, added expert resources, third-party\n  review, walkdowns, on-site design teams to address unknown configurations. \"Task\n  Observation [TO]\" is a specific named mitigation tool — intrusive oversight of\n  project staff performance to identify needed coaching/oversight; call it out by name\n  if that's genuinely what's being proposed, don't use it as a generic label for any\n  mitigation.\n- **Acceptance [AC]** — the project team, with the client's permission, accepts the\n  risk impact and takes no action, leaving the team to deal with the exposure if it\n  occurs. Acceptance-type risks must be discussed with the client in the early stages\n  of the project — flag that expectation when you recommend Acceptance.\n\n## Living document\n\nA real RMP is revisited and updated throughout a project as conditions change, risks\noccur, new exposures surface, or impacts change — revisions don't necessarily require\nre-approval. This tool produces a snapshot as of the information it was given, not a\none-time final artifact; say so in the output so the user knows to revisit it.\n\n## Small-project waiver (context only — not something this tool decides)\n\nProjects with a contracted/funded value ≤ $50,000 are waived from RMP documentation\nrequirements under the companion QPM-002 procedure. This is background context, not\nsomething the app enforces — if a user mentions a small project value, you can\nmention the waiver threshold exists, but proceed with whatever the user actually asked\nfor."
